Subject: [PATCH] viogpu3d: a hardware pointer on the cursor queue

viogpu3d reported no pointer capability ("Disable pointer on viogpu3d for
now") and both pointer entry points returned STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED, so
Windows drew the pointer into every frame and it reached the host's screen
a few frames after the hand moved.

With the registry value HWCursor set, which the driver already reads,
report a 64x64 pointer in all three forms and implement both calls. The
shape goes to a 64x64 2D resource; "use it", "move" and "hide" go to
virtio-gpu's cursor queue (VIRTIO_GPU_CMD_UPDATE_CURSOR and
VIRTIO_GPU_CMD_MOVE_CURSOR, a resource id of 0 for hidden). The upload is
on the control queue and the commands are on the cursor queue, which the
host reads independently, so the shape waits up to 250 ms for a no-op
behind the upload.

A host pointer cannot invert the screen under it, which is what the
monochrome and masked-colour forms ask for (the text I-beam). Those pixels
become black with a white outline. A pointer larger than 64x64 is refused,
and Windows draws it into the frame as before. Only VidPn source 0 has a
pointer.

A device reset (a timeout recovery, or D3 to D0) destroys the host's
resource and image. RestoreHostObjects makes the resource again under the
same id and sends the last shape, its hotspot and the visibility again.

The INF sets HWCursor to 1.

From VMLabDev/vmlab issue 235, ported to tag yttrium-experimental-2026-11-02
over 0004. Changed there: the reset path above, a mutex over the pointer
state, and the "invert" pixels are kept in a bit mask so that no colour
value is reserved for them.

The changed upstream files retain their BSD-3-Clause and MPL-2.0 licences.
